/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  solvent02 -- THE REAL CASE (DEV.md 4c C128, adjustment 2, step two).

  The target is polystyrene as Hansen placed it in his 1967 thesis (solute
  G, Table 4.1: centre (8.6, 3.0, 2.0) (cal/cm3)^0.5, radius 3.5); the
  solvents are three of his Table 2.1 placements, read from the page images
  and converted to Pa^0.5 in constant/components/*.dat, one citation per
  value.  Two blends, two lessons:

  1.  hexane + acetone, by VOLUME.  In the engine's MPa^0.5 (the thesis's
      (cal/cm3)^0.5 times 2.0455) polystyrene's radius is R0 = 7.159; pure
      hexane lies outside the sphere (Ra 9.238, RED 1.290) and pure acetone
      just inside its edge (Ra 6.638, RED 0.927 -- and rated 3, "strongly
      swollen, slight solubility", in the thesis's own test, Table 2.3).
      The blend dips well inside: the minimum is Ra 4.724 (RED 0.660) at
      65 % acetone by volume, and 50:50 gives 4.992 (RED 0.697).  The
      mixture rule lets two solvents on opposite sides of the target meet it
      between them, which is the observation Hansen checked his placements
      against (Section 2.5: "mixtures of non-solvents ... should ...
      dissolve the solute.  This has always been found to be the case").
      UNIFAC is asked whether the pair is one liquid at every point.

  2.  hexane + nitromethane, by MOLE (the basis conversion exercised: mole
      fractions become volume fractions through the records' Vliq, and the
      run prints both).  Both ends are far outside the sphere (RED 1.290
      and 1.850); the mixture rule puts the middle of the line inside (Ra
      5.399, RED 0.754 at x = 0.6, which is 38 % nitromethane by volume).
      But hexane and nitromethane are an immiscible pair at 25 C, and the
      case's UNIFAC is asked: where the Michelsen test finds a second liquid
      the op REFUSES to represent the pair as one solvent -- no averaged
      triple, no Ra, the point marked `twoPhase` -- and the minimum is taken
      over the points that survive.  Measured: 17 of 19 blends (x = 0.05 to
      0.85) are two-phase, every one where the rule would have put the blend
      inside the sphere among them, and the survivors all lie outside it --
      the minimum over survivors is pure hexane, RED 1.290.  The averaged
      number would have been the best on the line, and it describes a solvent
      that does not exist.  (A two-phase pair is not useless -- it may serve
      an extraction; it is only not one solvent.)

  T = 298.15 K is also every record's Tref, so no parameter is used away
  from the temperature it was placed at; the run says so either way.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

operations
(
    {
        name       hexaneAcetone;
        type       hansenMixture;
        target     polystyrene;
        solvents   ( nHexane acetone );
        composition
        {
            basis  volume;
            n      21;
        }
        T          298.15 K;
        output     { file "hansen_hexane_acetone.csv"; }
    }
    {
        name       hexaneNitromethane;
        type       hansenMixture;
        target     polystyrene;
        solvents   ( nHexane nitromethane );
        composition
        {
            basis  mole;                     // converted to volume through Vliq, printed
            n      21;
        }
        T          298.15 K;
        output     { file "hansen_hexane_nitromethane.csv"; }
    }
);
